v3.2.15
Radon Player 3.2.15: A DJ with more variety
## 3.2.15: A DJ with more variety (Windows and Android)
- **Nine new kinds of DJ set:** Artist spotlight (one artist you love, their big ones and a couple of deeper cuts), Album moment (three in a row from an album you play a lot, in the album's order), On this day (what you played this week one, two or more years ago), Decade trip (your songs from one decade, read from their tags, a different decade each time), One-hit wonders (artists you have only a song or two from), Never skipped (songs you've always heard to the end), Second chances (songs you usually skip, in case the mood's right today), Unreleased corner (songs in your Unreleased folders) and Streaks (songs you've played on four or more days this week). A kind that can't be made from your music (no Unreleased folder, say) is just left out.
- **More variety in every set:** no artist twice in a row, and at most two songs by one artist in a set (but for an artist's own sets). The kinds you haven't heard lately are likelier to come up next, and never the same kind twice in a row. Sets pick from all your music now, not just your top 300 songs: every song you've played (the most played still likelier) and every song of yours you haven't played yet, plus more of each year's and hour's.
- **Every set flows by mood:** the songs after the first are ordered by their moods, building up from a calm start and easing down from a loud one.
- **The DJ has more to say:** more lines for every kind of set; new facts (a song you've played every day this week, what you played a year ago this week, how long you've followed an artist, your big new artist this month, this month's plays, a song that fits your mood lately); milestones (a song's 100th play, its 500th…) always announced; greetings for Friday night, the weekend and Monday morning, and now and then a word about the day before a set.
- **A word between songs:** now and then, once a set at most, the DJ says something before a song in the middle of a set: that it's your number one, that you've never skipped it, that you only found it last week, or a milestone it's reaching. It's made while the song before plays, so it's said straight away (that song ends without a crossfade).
- **YouTube songs download in FLAC:** a song from YouTube Music is often its music video, a little longer than the recording, and the match on Tidal turned down anything more than 6 seconds off, so it downloaded from YouTube (Opus 160 kbps) instead. Now a YouTube song may be up to half a minute off when its title and artist match closely, so it comes from Tidal in FLAC. The same match is used when a song streams, so the DJ's new finds from YouTube Music play from Tidal in FLAC too (YouTube only when Tidal really hasn't the song).
- **YouTube downloads are AAC, not Opus:** a song only YouTube has is saved as its best AAC (.m4a, 256 kbps where YouTube offers it, else 128), which plays and tags everywhere like your other M4As; Opus only when YouTube has no AAC of it.
- **Upgrade in Downloads:** your YouTube downloads got again from Tidal in FLAC where Tidal has them, else (the Opus ones) as YouTube's AAC; each old file is deleted once the new one is in.
- **Downloads stay in Downloads:** when the app started, a download the phone wouldn't open for a moment (a permission or a busy file provider) was taken off the list, though its file was still there. Only one whose file is really gone is taken off now.
- **Crossfade without a cut:** the ending song began fading the moment the next one was asked for, but the next only came up once it had loaded, so for that moment both were low, which sounded like a cut. Now the ending plays on at full until the next song is really playing, and then one fade takes the ending down as the next comes up, in step (equal power, so it never dips). Skipping during a crossfade stops the ending at once.
- **Car always wins (Settings › Playback, under Newest Bluetooth device wins, the phone):** a failsafe: whenever the car is connected (its Bluetooth or Android Auto), it has the music and its EQ, whatever headphones are connected too. The app learns the car's Bluetooth name the first time Android Auto starts with it (and knows names that are plainly a car's). Ignore Bluetooth devices still comes first.
- **Newest Bluetooth device wins sticks with the car:** getting in the car with an earbud set down, the music went to the car, then back to the earbud a couple of seconds later, for two reasons: Android Auto starting a moment after the car's Bluetooth counted as a newer device, and an earbud dropping out for a moment and coming back counted as newly connected. Now Android Auto and the car's Bluetooth are one device, and a device back within a minute and a half keeps the time it first came, so the car keeps the music.
- **Switch it up from your headphones:** while the DJ plays, tap your headphones four times (two double taps, which they send as two Nexts) and it switches it up instead of skipping twice. One double tap still skips one song. Works on the phone and on the PC.
- **Refresh in Downloads:** ↻ Refresh beside the sort reads your downloads again, so one that didn't show up comes in.

### Good to know
- **Android:** install RadonPlayer.apk over 3.2.14 (same signature, your library and settings stay), or tap Update in Settings.
- **Windows:** Radon-Player-3.2.15.msi installs over 3.2.14, or use Update in Settings.
